 "text": {
  "measured": "Field teams from the Geological Survey of Japan taped the sideways offset across the break at 9 places along it, from 17 cm to 184 cm (the largest, 184 cm, across a road in Hikawa). GPS stations on the ground measured how far the land moved at 8 points around the break, from 2 cm to 88 cm, the most at Senchō, 2.5 km north-west of it.",
  "drawn": "Everything between those points is filled in by a rule. The two sides slide past each other by exactly the surveyed offset at each place, and by a straight-line blend between places. The offset shrinks to nothing over the last 2 km at each end of the line, so the tear closes. The 3 sites within 2 km of an end of the line (23 cm, 25 cm, 17 cm) fall inside that closing and are shown smaller. Each side moves along the line, the north-west side north-east and the south-east side south-west. The north-west side takes 78% of the offset and the other side the rest. The movement fades with distance from the line, to half its size at 16 km and to 42% of its size at 20 km. The share and the fading were fitted to the 7 GPS stations along the drawn line.",
  "caveat": "NISAR did not measure this movement. One radar pass only sees ground moving toward or away from the satellite, so it cannot say which way along the ground the two sides slid. This picture comes from the survey and the GPS. The model is typically 9 cm away from the GPS at the 7 stations it was fitted to, and the biggest miss over all 8 is 40 cm at Jōnan (past the end of the drawn line, where the break was found only by town name). Up-and-down movement is not drawn."
 },
